/// A floating-point literal may carry an exponent.
///
/// There is no exponent notation today: 1.1e-9 scans as 1.1, then the
/// identifier e, then minus, then 9.
///
/// ⚠️ This one costs the emitter nothing, and the evidence is already in the
/// build.  VisitLiteral emits alg_double(Str(Value)) -- the value re-rendered,
/// never the lexeme -- and Str already writes E notation, so the literal
/// 1000000000.0 reaches C today as alg_double(1.0E9).  The path is exercised on
/// every build; exponent literals only let a program reach it directly.
///
/// An exponent alone makes a literal a Double, with no fractional part needed,
/// which is why 1e9 is asserted against a Double rather than an Integer.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after arguments.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
test 'A floating-point literal may carry an exponent';
begin
    AssertEqual (0.0000000011, 1.1e-9);
    AssertEqual (1000000000.0, 1e9);
    AssertEqual (1000000000.0, 1E9);
    AssertEqual (1500.0, 1.5e+3);
    AssertTrue  (1e9 is Double);
end
